CH = dict(num=5, tag="History", theme="From woodblocks to the modern press: books, ideas, reform and nationalism",
 title="Print Culture and the Modern World",
 intro="Print shaped the modern world. It made books cheap, carried new ideas to ordinary readers, made reform debates public and helped nationalism spread. This chapter follows print from East Asia to Europe and then India, and shows how governments and readers reacted.",
 big_questions=["How did printing begin and spread?", "How did print change religion and ideas in Europe?", "How did print develop in India?", "How did print affect reform, women and nationalism?", "How did the British try to control it?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "The First Printed Books"),
 ("ul", ["Print technology began in **China, Japan and Korea** with **hand-printing** from woodblocks. China had a huge civil service examination system, and the state was the major producer of printed material. The Buddhist **Diamond Sutra**, printed in **868 CE**, is the oldest dated printed book to survive.", "**Marco Polo** brought the knowledge of woodblock printing from China to Italy in **1295**. Books were printed on vellum; the wealthy bought hand-written books, while woodblock printed books spread in Europe.", "In Japan, printing of the 17th-century city culture covered urban life, with illustrated books on women, music, tea ceremony and cooking."]),
 ("h2", "Print Comes to Europe: Gutenberg"),
 ("ul", ["**Johann Gutenberg**, a goldsmith from Strasbourg (Germany), developed the first known printing press in the **1430s** at his family farm in Mainz, using the olive and wine press as a model, and metal moulds for the alphabet. The first book he printed was the **Bible**: about **180 copies in three years**, which was fast by the standards of the day.", "By **1450-1550** printing presses were set up in most countries of Europe; in the second half of the fifteenth century, about 20 million copies of printed books were produced in Europe, and in the sixteenth century it went up to about 200 million. This was the **print revolution**.", "Book prices fell; readers increased; new reading public emerged; people memorised and heard stories read aloud, but now **reading** took over."]),
 ("h3", "Religious debates and the fear of print"),
 ("ul", ["**Martin Luther** wrote the **Ninety-Five Theses** (1517) criticising the Roman Catholic Church. It was printed and widely read, leading to the **Protestant Reformation**. Luther said print was ‘God's ultimate gift’.", "An Italian miller, **Menocchio**, read books and reinterpreted the Bible; the Church tried him and he was executed. The Church imposed controls on publishers and booksellers and began to maintain an **Index of Prohibited Books** from **1558**."]),
 ("h2", "The Reading Mania"),
 ("ul", ["By the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries literacy rose in Europe; peasants and artisans began to read. Literacy grew through church schools and popular ballads and folk tales, and in some regions by 1800 reached 60 to 80 per cent.", "Cheap booklets called **chapbooks**, sold by pedlars (chapmen), and ‘penny’ books reached the poor; **almanacs**, ballads and newspapers circulated.", "In the nineteenth century new reading groups appeared: children, women and workers; ‘penny magazines’ and **serialised novels** by writers such as **Charles Dickens**; circulating libraries; women like **Jane Austen, the Brontë sisters and George Eliot** wrote and became a new class of readers."]),
 ("h3", "Technical innovations"),
 ("p", "In the late 18th century, metal presses replaced wooden ones. By the mid-nineteenth century, **Richard M. Hoe** of New York perfected the **power-driven cylindrical press**, which could print 8,000 sheets an hour, and the **offset press** later printed six colours. Electrically operated presses, automatic paper reels and a series of other changes sped up production; printers promoted their products with book covers, jackets and illustrations."),
 ("h3", "Print and the French Revolution"),
 ("p", "Many historians argue that print created a climate of thought: books by **Voltaire and Rousseau** promoted reason and criticised custom; print popularised the ideas of **Enlightenment** thinkers; a spirit of questioning of authority arose; cheap satire and cartoons mocked the monarchy; **Mercier** declared, ‘Tremble, therefore, tyrants of the world!’"),
 ("h2", "India and the World of Print"),
 ("ul", ["India had a rich tradition of handwritten manuscripts in Sanskrit, Arabic, Persian and vernacular languages, copied on palm leaf or handmade paper; they were expensive and fragile and not accessible to ordinary people.", "**Portuguese missionaries** brought the printing press to **Goa** in the mid-sixteenth century; by **1674** about 50 books were printed in Konkani and Kannada. Catholic priests printed the first **Tamil** book in 1579 at Cochin and the first Malayalam book in 1713. Dutch Protestant missionaries printed Tamil texts.", "**James Augustus Hickey** began the weekly *Bengal Gazette* in **1780**, a magazine that was proud of being ‘open to all parties, but influenced by none’; he attacked the Company's officials. The Governor-General Warren Hastings persecuted him. **Gangadhar Bhattacharya** later brought out the first Indian-owned weekly, also called *Bengal Gazette*."]),
 ("h3", "Religious reform and public debates"),
 ("ul", ["Debates about widow immolation (sati), monotheism, Brahmanical priesthood and idolatry involved reformers such as **Rammohan Roy**, who published the *Sambad Kaumudi* (**1821**), and conservatives such as the *Samachar Chandrika*.", "Muslim clerics of **Deoband** (the Deoband seminary, founded in 1867) published thousands of **fatwas** telling Muslims how to conduct themselves; Hindu devotional texts such as the Ramcharitmanas of Tulsidas were printed cheaply from the 1810s."]),
 ("h3", "New forms of publication"),
 ("ul", ["Novels, lyrics, short stories, essays about social issues and political subjects arose; painters like **Raja Ravi Varma** produced mass-circulated prints for homes; caricatures and cartoons appeared in journals from the 1870s.", "**Women** became readers and writers: Rashsundari Debi's *Amar Jiban* (**1876**), a Bengali autobiography, **Tarabai Shinde** wrote *Stripurushtulna* (1882) attacking social differences. Not everyone approved, and some fathers and husbands banned women from reading.", "**Social reformers:** **Jyotiba Phule** wrote *Gulamgiri* (c. 1871) on caste injustice; **Dr B. R. Ambedkar** and **E. V. Ramaswamy Naicker (Periyar)** wrote powerful critiques; mill workers such as **Kashibaba**, a Kanpur mill worker, wrote *Chhote aur Bade Ka Sawal* (1938) to show links between caste and class exploitation. **Libraries** in towns gave workers access to books."]),
 ("h2", "Print and Censorship"),
 ("ul", ["In its early years the East India Company was not much concerned about censorship, except for Anglo-Indian papers critical of the Company. After the 1857 revolt, attitudes changed: ‘native’ papers were seen as dangerous.", "The **Vernacular Press Act (1878)**, modelled on Irish press laws, gave the government the right to censor reports and editorials in the vernacular press; if a paper published a report judged ‘seditious’, the paper was warned, then press and equipment seized.", "In spite of this, **nationalist newspapers** grew in numbers; when Punjab revolutionaries were deported in 1907, **Balgangadhar Tilak** wrote with great sympathy in his *Kesari*, and was imprisoned in 1908. This provoked protests."]),
 ("table", ["Date", "Event"], [["868 CE", "Buddhist Diamond Sutra, oldest dated printed book"], ["1295", "Marco Polo brings woodblock knowledge to Italy"], ["1430s", "Gutenberg's press"], ["1517", "Luther's Ninety-Five Theses"], ["1558", "Index of Prohibited Books"], ["1780", "Hickey's Bengal Gazette"], ["1821", "Sambad Kaumudi"], ["1878", "Vernacular Press Act"]]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Who developed the printing press in Europe?", "Johann Gutenberg, in the 1430s."),
  ("What was the Protestant Reformation?", "Movement against Church practices begun by Luther's 95 Theses (1517)."),
  ("What were chapbooks?", "Small cheap books sold by pedlars to common readers."),
  ("Who started the Bengal Gazette?", "James Augustus Hickey, in 1780."),
  ("What was the Vernacular Press Act?", "1878 law letting the government censor Indian-language newspapers."),
  ("How did print help social reform?", "It carried the writings of Phule, Ambedkar and others to wide readers."),
  ("Who wrote Amar Jiban?", "Rashsundari Debi, 1876."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Printing began with **woodblocks in East Asia**; **Gutenberg's press** (1430s) began the print revolution in Europe.", "Print spread the **Reformation**, Enlightenment ideas and a wide new reading public.", "In India print came with **missionaries** and became a tool for **reform and nationalism**.", "Women, workers and lower castes found a voice in print.", "The government answered with controls such as the **Vernacular Press Act (1878)**."])
